            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for beef, then the Mandarin for chicken meat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: yìbān",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "yìbān — \"generally; as a rule; ordinary\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ yìbān qī diǎn qǐchuáng — As a rule I get up at seven."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The tone of 一 changes: yī is said yì before the first tone of bān."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "yìbān is built from 一 'one' and bān 'kind'. The first is inherited from Middle Chinese 'jit (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the second is listed there as pan, glossed 'to carry', and as ban, glossed 'joy'. The sense 'kind' is not given, so how the pair came to mean 'as a rule' is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for times of day."
            }
          ]
        },
